riends here he was happy he  wasn't motivated to improve his accent  which is fine okay however and i've  noticed that very often students  who haven't had  nearly the amount of exposure for  example that my father had they will  often pronounce better because they're  more motivated to improve their  pronunciation and so therefore they  become more attentive to how things are  pronounced they're more motivated to  blend in with the culture  and as a result they end up so they're  more deliberate about it they want to do  it more they  like the culture that they're learning  they end up pronouncing  better  so attitude time and being attentive now  the nuts and bolts and i said in my  previous video that you need to do a lot  of listening to material that has  resonance  i found that even though i lived in  france for three years  uh  my pronunciation in french actually  improved the last few years because i  was  i started enjoying french audiobooks so  i would be i can still remember  uh jogging while listening to uh le  memoir dadrean  by uh marguerite your sonar or uh  i love balzac or i was gardening while  listening to uh liaison dangerous  for example i like classical literature  not everyone does  and  i'm listening to it and i'm i'm caught  up in it just like with italian  listening to i promise esposi or another  great recording by il naratore you  should look them up ilnaratore  punto.com on the internet great source  of audiobooks in italian or in italian  rai has this aleotto de la sera  series on history and so listening to  all this stuff  really improved my pronunciation because  i was focusing on not only the content  but also getting that rhythm of the  language and i think the rhythm of the  language the intonation  is something that uh  you know students of linguistics  sometimes called prosody or something i  don't know that term to me it's just  intonation rhythm  that's much more important than  certain particular sounds  um every language is 