on link  which is a story that we have in all  however many languages  and the word money comes up all the time  or i noticed another word veglum de  vigne i haven't really focused in on it  but in russian vignette  looks viglonde  so i'm picking up on you know little  bits and pieces that start to suck into  place but i'm not worried about it  uh  i'm just allowing that big picture to  flow over me  uh there are rules that can help you for  example the  ah you know if you have e n in french is  pronounced ah most of the time but when  it comes  after an i or a y  uh it's  uh like adrian  uh or uh vegetarian  and so you may not notice that and you  might say adrian vegetarian  and so then if you've had all this  exposure and you see a rule that reminds  you  that in fact it's vegetarian  uh  then that's going to make sense to you  but the idea that before you start out  into a language you can have  all the sounds that they that you can  hear them all that you can make them all  and uh that you know when you know  it's pronounced this way or that way  because not all languages are are  consistent again going to french you  know  donk the c is pronounced but yet very  often the the last consonant is not  pronounced so  all i'm saying is that uh i should  stress too that for people who find  the bits and pieces  the international phonetic alphabet and  so forth uh or specific rules who like  to study that way by all means i'm not  by any means suggesting people shouldn't  do that i always talk about what my  experience is and my experience has been  that that's kind of a distraction  i want to expose myself to as much  of the overall the big picture as  possible and later on there are things  that i'll miss and at that point to go  in and review some rules on  pronunciation probably is a good thing  it's the same as with grammar i can't  possibly  learn all the fundamental rules of  grammar up front and then proceed to  learn the language i first have to get  that massive exposure to the language  and then you know periodically go in and  look at some rules of grammar and now  those rules relate to something um  you know i've had so many experiences of  of people who get corrected on the  pronunciation some specific aspect of  the pronunciation and they go right back  to making the same mistake so  i stress again that  with regard to  uh pronunciation  you have to have that attitude you want  to improve your pronunciation you want  to be one of them  you're prepared to listen a lot to  what is to you attractive content to try  to notice things to try to imitate them  you can always uh i'll finish and then  take the phone call  just a sec here  hello  hello  hello hey walter can i call you in five  minutes  will do thanks bye  that's my carpenter  so before i lose my train of thought  here  nothing against the international  phonetic alphabet i don't use it  uh i find it not useful if i can't  pronounce something you know the polish  l it seems like it's a oh of some some  kind you know uh  yeah i'm gonna have to get used to it  whatever the phonetic symbol for that is  almost doesn't matter to me  i i know i'm hearing it now it's a bit  of a more open l i gotta get used to it  uh but if others find that useful by all  means  however there's no point you know i  don't get the complete dismissal of the  phonetic alphabet all i said was i don't  find it that useful  other people do by all means go for it  uh what else was i gonna say here yeah  and if i can't make the sound or hear  the sound the phonetic alphabet isn't  going to help me however it's as i say  it's possible that i miss things  and at that point some form of attention  to detail which might include the  international phonetic alphabet if you  have learned it  might be helpful  uh there was also a question from one of  my viewers who is working on french and  is studying at university and what can  he do i think was that he  uh  to work on french because he is going 